#{[#edb/ref 17592186050240 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074706} :activity/id #uuid "d488bf85-6fde-58c0-8209-525fccfed6ea" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061 :tutorial/content "The systems of equations we've encountered up to now have all had *exactly one solution*.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x + 1; y = 1 - x\n$$\n\nUsing either substitution or elimination, we can show that the solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $(0,1)$. This is the *only* solution, and it tells us the point of intersection between the two corresponding lines.\n\n![](images/bd/bd7edb530e16b853318a3e38785c7b6353c1b54f10ee3c4e2de7da915450f6d9.png)\n\nIn general, systems of equations can have\n\n- *exactly one* solution (like the example above)\n- *infinitely many* solutions\n- *no solutions*\n\nTo determine the number of solutions to a system of equations, we solve it using either substitution or elimination and interpret the result.\n\nLet's look at a system of equations with infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075063 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2; 2 \\cdot (3x - y) = 2 \\cdot (-2); 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*." :tutorial/title "A System With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480 :question/id #uuid "f2105fec-c85b-5928-92e6-95ed80f1326c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n${4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{4x + 2y = 4; 2x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075049 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n{a_{1}x + b_{1}y = c_{1}; a_{2}x + b_{2}y = c_{2}\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n| ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png) | ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png) | ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines. Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point. Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide. Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." :tutorial/title "The Intersection of Two Lines"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481 :question/id #uuid "b1d34db3-7f30-5a8c-b233-fd955ac656f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the lines $x - 2y = 2$ and $10y - 5x = -10$ intersect?" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 2; 10y - 5x =-10\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075062 :tutorial/content "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$. To do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2⇒y = 3x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number." :tutorial/title "Describing General Solutions of Systems With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075064 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n{3x - y =-2; 6x - 2y =-4\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y)|= (0, 3(0) + 2); = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n${3(0) - 2 =-2; 6(0) - 2(2) =-4$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y)|= (1, 3(1) + 2); = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n${3(1) - 5 =-2; 6(1) - 2(5) =-4$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." :tutorial/title "Understanding the Result"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482 :question/id #uuid "ab09c3eb-6813-57c6-891b-bf20196cbe93" :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $3x+2y=2$ and $6x+4y=4$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2; 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"}}] :activity/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073927}] [#edb/ref 17592186050245 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504} :activity/id #uuid "16a4de81-848b-5638-b998-ac9062204da4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088989 :tutorial/content "Two lines that never cross are said to be **parallel**. For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nFor example, the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2$, so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087433 :question/id #uuid "4f0593d8-bce5-5c73-aedd-ffe0faf15a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-14364" :question/problem "The lines $y=cx+7$ and $y=5x-3$ are parallel. What is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c$.\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5$."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087434 :question/id #uuid "b2f31785-e173-518c-a1fd-b069e0834b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14367" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3$?\n\n1. $y=4x-8$\n2. $y=12x+1$\n3. $y=3x-12$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - y &= 3 \\\\\n- y &= -12x + 3 \\\\\ny &= 12x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is\n\n$$\ny = 12x+1\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Standard Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087688 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087435 :question/id #uuid "88c5f90c-9bf9-58a2-a9a2-7c7ff1fc4f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14370" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$kx - 2y|=-1; - 2y|=-kx - 1; 2y|= kx + 1; y|= \\frac{k}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{2}=2\n$$\n\nConsequently, $k=4$."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters"}}] :activity/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087910}] [#edb/ref 17592186050163 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101854} :activity/id #uuid "8d861dcd-02c5-53fa-858a-2be900cad05a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101952 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe denote the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}3$rd row and $\\color{red}2$nd column using the notation $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}.$\n\nThe first subscript represents the row and the second subscript represents the column. So $a_{{\\color{blue}3}\\color{red}2}=8,$ as shown below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{8} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can denote every entry of a matrix in this way.\nFor instance, $a_{{\\color{blue}4}\\color{red}1}$ denotes the entry that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}4$th row and $\\color{red}1$st column.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33} \\\\ {a}_{41} & {a}_{42} & {a}_{43}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100683 :question/id #uuid "622baf41-fe35-55ee-bfc6-f1e6a2cb089a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4371" :question/problem "Find the entry $a_{23}$ of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $a_{23}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $3$rd column. Therefore, $a_{23}={\\color{blue}{-3}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100989 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100684 :question/id #uuid "fe56a988-49e9-5f33-b0a5-f43d33984c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4372" :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix ${B},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of a Square Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101953 :tutorial/content "In general, we denote the entry of a matrix $A$ that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}i$th row and $\\color{red}j$th column by $a_{{\\color{blue}i}\\color{red}j}.$\n\nIf $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, we can write it as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & … & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & … & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & … & {a}_{mn}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere each entry $a_{ij}$ is defined for $i = 1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j =1,2,\\ldots,n.$" :tutorial/title "General Form of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100685 :question/id #uuid "432a6a1a-ba9d-5aa0-b758-d4d32e9992f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4373" :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $C$ given below. Describe it in terms of $c_{ij}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 34 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry lies at the intersection of the $1$st row and $3$rd column. Therefore, we have $c_{13}={\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5}}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}}] :activity/title "Index Notation for Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101034}] [#edb/ref 17592186050190 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457} :activity/id #uuid "2acc7e09-91cc-5ff3-8a20-8051f891cc61" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193941 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; x - y =-2\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables.\n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(x + 2y = 1; (x - y =-2)\\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x$, allowing us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- x + y &= 2 \\\\\n(2y + y) &= (1 + 2) \\\\\n3y &= 3 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -2 \\\\\nx - (1) &= -2 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1, 1)$." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :question/id #uuid "c700cc22-203a-5ad3-913c-e5eb507e22e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${a + 4b = 1; 2a + b =-5$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n{(a + 4b = 1)\\cdot (-2); 2a + b =-5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a - 8b &= -2 \\\\\n2a + b &= -5 \\\\\n(-8b + b) &= (-2 - 5) \\\\\n- 7b &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192586 :question/id #uuid "eb7f1f32-1a5c-526e-878b-8046c4feb8c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13997" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${3x + 4y = 2; x - 2y = 4$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(3x + 4y = 2; (x - 2y = 4)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n(3x + 2x) &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :question/id #uuid "f20f46d9-b799-57a2-90bc-12e6593e8655" :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${2x + 3y = 8; x - 2y =-3$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(2x + 3y = 8; (x - 2y =-3) \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 8 \\\\\n- 2x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\n(3y + 4y) &= (8 + 6) \\\\\n7y &= 14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= -3 \\\\\nx - 2(2) &= -3 \\\\\nx - 4 &= -3 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2$, which can be expressed as $(1,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :question/id #uuid "990e3660-dfd7-50fa-b60b-0478ff02a073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${5u - 6v = 3; - 3u + 2v =-5$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3$, then the $v$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{5u - 6v = 3; (-3u + 2v =-5) \\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5u - 6v &= 3 \\\\\n- 9u + 6v &= -15 \\\\\n(5u - 9u) &= (3 - 15) \\\\\n- 4u &= -12 \\\\\nu &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3$. However, we still need to find $v$. To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 3(3) + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 9 + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n2v &= 4 \\\\\nv &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nu = 3, \\,v = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192905}]}
